namespace DGtal {
/*!
@page moduleVisibilityLattice Visibility lattice estimation
@writers Romain Negro

[TOC]

Part of the \ref packageGeometry.

\section moduleVisibilityLattice_sec1 Purpose

This module provides visibility-based geometric estimators on pointel sets
through lattice matching. It computes:

- a visibility relation between pointels and primitive directions;
- normal vectors from this visibility relation, using weighted local covariance.

The method is described in the DGMM 2025 proceedings
@cite NegroLachaudDGMM2025 and in its convergence-focused JMIV article
@cite NegroLachaudJMIV2026. The latter is part of the
<a href="https://link.springer.com/journal/10851/volumes-and-issues/68-5">
JMIV special issue, volume 68, issue 5</a>.

\section moduleVisibilityLattice_sec1_visibility What visibility means

Let @f$X@f$ be a digital shape and @f$p,q@f$ two of its pointels. They are
visible in @f$X@f$ when the cells touched by the segment @f$[p,q]@f$ all
belong to the cubical star of @f$X@f$. In other words, the segment can follow
the boundary's immediate digital neighborhood, but cannot cut across a gap
or pass through the interior of a salient feature. Visibility is not
necessarily connected: visible pointels from a given point may be separated
in the digital adjacency graph.

The estimator computes visibility exactly up to a requested radius. It
represents the cubical star as a lattice map: for each projection onto the
chosen main axis, the occupied lattice coordinates are stored as ordered
integer intervals. For each primitive direction whose infinity norm is at
most the radius, translated intervals are intersected to determine all visible
segments. This interval matching does not rely on traversing a connected
visible neighborhood and works in arbitrary dimension.

\section moduleVisibilityLattice_sec2_normals Visibility-based normal estimation

For a pointel @f$p@f$, the normal estimator considers only pointels visible
from @f$p@f$ and within the selected neighborhood radius. A Gaussian weight
with scale @f$\\sigma@f$ can down-weight distant pointels; other available
kernel choices are \c Ring and \c None. The weighted centroid of the
neighborhood (or @f$p@f$ itself) is used as the covariance center. The
estimated normal direction is the eigenvector associated with the smallest
eigenvalue of the weighted covariance matrix.

Restricting the neighborhood by visibility helps retain sharp features and
gaps that an ordinary Euclidean neighborhood can mix together. On digitized
smooth shapes, the article reports convergence behavior as the grid is
refined; the JMIV article develops the corresponding convergence analysis.
The eigenvector's sign is not oriented by this API, so applications that need
oriented normals must orient the returned vectors separately.

\section moduleVisibilityLattice_sec3 API and parameters

The main implementation is:

- VisibilityLatticeEstimator.

\subsection moduleVisibilityLattice_sec3_api Main API

For a given space \c TSpace:

- VisibilityLatticeEstimator<TSpace>::getVisibility computes the visibility relation;
- VisibilityLatticeEstimator<TSpace>::getNormalVisibility computes normals from a precomputed visibility;
- VisibilityLatticeEstimator<TSpace>::getVisibilityNormalVectors computes normals from a custom visibility predicate.

The corresponding \ref ShortcutsGeometry convenience methods also accept
DGtal \c Parameters objects. The visibility computation is controlled by
\c radius (maximum primitive-direction infinity norm), \c nStar (number of star-cover
dilations), and \c gridstep (used to infer a radius when \c radius is \c -1).
Normal estimation uses \c sigma for the weighting kernel,
\c neighborhood-radius for the spatial cutoff, \c auto-limiter and
\c auto-limiter-ratio to trim distant visible pointels, and
\c visibility-center and \c visibility-kernel for the center and weight
policies.

Kernel and center choices are available through:

- VisibilityLatticeEstimator<TSpace>::VisibilityKernel;
- VisibilityLatticeEstimator<TSpace>::VisibilityCenter.

\section moduleVisibilityLattice_sec4 Example

The \ref geometry/meshes/visibility-lattice-normals-goursat.cpp example
demonstrates the estimator on a digitized implicit Goursat shape and exports
an OBJ vector field. The
\ref geometry/meshes/visibility-lattice-normals-from-volume.cpp example
instead reads a binary volume and writes point positions and normals as
six-column text rows.

Both examples:

- extract pointels from a 3D digital surface, generated from a Goursat shape
  or read from a volume;
- compute visibility with VisibilityLatticeEstimator;
- estimate and export visibility-based normals.

@see geometry/meshes/visibility-lattice-normals-goursat.cpp
@see geometry/meshes/visibility-lattice-normals-from-volume.cpp
*/
}
